# streaming-availability

A Python client for the [Streaming Availability API](https://docs.movieofthenight.com)
(movieofthenight.com) — look up streaming availability for movies and series
across Netflix, Disney+, Apple TV, Max, Hulu and more, in 66 countries.

- **Sync by default**, built on [`requests`](https://requests.readthedocs.io/) — zero extra setup.
- **Optional async support**, built on [`httpx`](https://www.python-httpx.org/) — install the `async` extra if you want it.
- Typed response models (`Show`, `Country`, `Genre`, `Change`, ...) with `.raw` always available as an escape hatch.
- One place (`StreamingAvailabilityError` and subclasses) to catch API errors.

## Installation

```bash
# sync only (uses requests)
pip install streaming-availability

# with async support (also installs httpx)
pip install streaming-availability[async]
```

You'll need an API key from either
[developers.movieofthenight.com](https://developers.movieofthenight.com/) or
[RapidAPI](https://rapidapi.com/movie-of-the-night-movie-of-the-night-default/api/streaming-availability/pricing).

## Quick start (sync)

```python
from streaming_availability import StreamingAvailabilityClient

client = StreamingAvailabilityClient(api_key="YOUR_API_KEY")

show = client.get_show("tt0111161")  # also accepts TMDB ids
print(show.title, show.release_year)

for option in show.streaming_options.get("us", []):
    print(option.service.name, option.type, option.link)
```

If your key is from RapidAPI instead, just add `use_rapidapi=True`:

```python
client = StreamingAvailabilityClient(api_key="YOUR_RAPIDAPI_KEY", use_rapidapi=True)
```

`StreamingAvailabilityClient` also works as a context manager, which closes
its underlying `requests.Session` for you:

```python
with StreamingAvailabilityClient(api_key="YOUR_API_KEY") as client:
    results = client.search_shows_by_title(title="The Matrix", country="us")
```

## Quick start (async)

Install the extra first: `pip install streaming-availability[async]`.

```python
import asyncio
from streaming_availability import AsyncStreamingAvailabilityClient

async def main():
    async with AsyncStreamingAvailabilityClient(api_key="YOUR_API_KEY") as client:
        show = await client.get_show("tt0111161")
        print(show.title)

asyncio.run(main())
```

The async client mirrors the sync client method-for-method — same names,
same arguments, same return types — so you can mix it into an existing
`asyncio`/`httpx`-based project alongside your other API clients without
the sync client ever blocking your event loop. If you try to use
`AsyncStreamingAvailabilityClient` without `httpx` installed, you'll get a
clear `ImportError` telling you to install the extra, rather than a
confusing import failure.

## Endpoints

Every method below exists on both `StreamingAvailabilityClient` (sync) and
`AsyncStreamingAvailabilityClient` (async, `await` the call).

| Method | Description |
|---|---|
| `get_countries(output_language=None)` | All supported countries and their services |
| `get_country(country_code, output_language=None)` | A single country |
| `get_genres(output_language=None)` | All genres |
| `get_show(show_id, country=None, series_granularity=None, output_language=None)` | A show by id / IMDb id / TMDB id |
| `search_shows_by_title(title, country, show_type=None, series_granularity=None, output_language=None)` | Search by title |
| `search_shows_by_filters(country, catalogs=None, show_type=None, genres=None, genres_relation=None, ...)` | Search by genre/year/rating/catalog filters, paginated |
| `get_top_shows(country, service, output_language=None, show_type=None)` | Top shows on a given service |
| `get_changes(country, change_type, item_type, ...)` | Recent/upcoming catalog changes, paginated |

### Pagination

`search_shows_by_filters` and `get_changes` return a result with `.has_more`
and `.next_cursor`. Pass `next_cursor` back in as `cursor=...` to get the
next page:

```python
result = client.search_shows_by_filters(country="us", genres=["action"])
all_shows = list(result.shows)

while result.has_more:
    result = client.search_shows_by_filters(
        country="us", genres=["action"], cursor=result.next_cursor
    )
    all_shows.extend(result.shows)
```

### Enums

String-valued parameters (`show_type`, `change_type`, `item_type`,
`order_direction`, etc.) accept either a plain string (e.g. `"movie"`) or
the corresponding enum from `streaming_availability.enums`
(e.g. `ShowType.MOVIE`) — whichever you prefer:

```python
from streaming_availability import ShowType

client.search_shows_by_title(title="Dune", country="us", show_type=ShowType.MOVIE)
# is equivalent to:
client.search_shows_by_title(title="Dune", country="us", show_type="movie")
```

## Error handling

All errors raised by this library inherit from `StreamingAvailabilityError`,
which carries `.status_code` and `.response_body`:

```python
from streaming_availability import NotFoundError, StreamingAvailabilityError

try:
    client.get_show("not-a-real-id")
except NotFoundError:
    print("No such show")
except StreamingAvailabilityError as e:
    print(f"API error ({e.status_code}): {e.message}")
```

Specific subclasses: `BadRequestError` (400), `AuthenticationError` (401/403),
`NotFoundError` (404), `RateLimitError` (429), `ServerError` (5xx).

## Working with response models

Responses are wrapped in small typed classes rather than raw dicts, but the
raw JSON is always available via `.raw` / `.to_dict()` in case the API
returns a field this library doesn't know about yet:

```python
show = client.get_show("tt0111161")
show.title          # typed property
show.raw["title"]   # same value, straight from the JSON
```

